01M4WH1D6M7NK85TK2FT61GZA3 high firmware/src/dcache.h 14
dcache_set_line_size() (firmware/src/dcache.h) disables interrupts only on the calling core (noInterrupts() is per-core on ESP32) while it suspends, reconfigures and invalidates the shared data cache. But it's invoked from hackStarted() (main.cpp:510), which runs on every hack switch and at boot — after buttonTask has already been started pinned to core 0 (main.cpp:621, xTaskCreatePinnedToCore(..., 0)). If that other core touches any flash- or PSRAM-backed data while the D-cache is suspended/invalidated on this core, it can crash or read corrupted data — this is why ESP-IDF's own flash-write path (which does similar Cache_Suspend/Resume calls) stops the other CPU via IPC first. The new diag/psram_bench.h version of this same pattern is only ever run at boot before buttonTask exists ("before the other core does much", per its own comment), i.e. its authors already recognized the hazard and avoided it there — but the production call site in hackStarted() has no such guard, and fires repeatedly during normal operation (every rotation and button press), not just once at boot.

01M4WGYZB36DTW0T0XAP29TNHV high firmware/src/dcache.h 16
Quiesce the other core before flushing and reconfiguring the shared data cache. `noInterrupts()` only disables interrupts on the calling core, while core 0 remains active (including the button task started before `hackStarted()`). A flash-rodata or PSRAM access there while DCache is suspended can cause a cache-disabled access fault; PSRAM writes between `Cache_WriteBack_All()` and suspension can also be discarded by the subsequent invalidation. This affects normal boot and rotations that change cache mode. Use an ESP-IDF-style cross-core rendezvous that parks the other CPU in cache-safe code before writeback and keeps it parked until the cache is resumed; apply the same protection to the benchmark helper.

